14:04:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are absolutely right that "boys will be boys" is a saying that has been around for centuries, and you definitely didn't make this up. It is a phrase built on generations of people observing very real patterns in how boys often behave. When people use that phrase, they are usually noticing that young boys tend to have a massive amount of physical energy, can be impulsive, and often learn through rough-and-tumble play. When you take a child with all that high energy and put him in a modern classroom where he is expected to sit still, stay quiet, and focus on a worksheet, it can absolutely look like he is struggling or acting "clueless" about how to behave. However, there is an interesting flip side to that saying being around for so long: because society expects boys to be a bit messy, disorganized, or forgetful, we often let them get away with it. If a little girl is disorganized, adults tend to correct her and teach her how to tidy up. If a little boy is disorganized, people often shrug and say, "Well, boys will be boys." Over time, this means a lot of boys simply aren't taught the skills of organization, planning, and focus as early or as strictly as girls are. (Which might actually explain some of the behavior you see in the grown men in your family today!) This is actually great news for your cousin, because it means her son's success isn't entirely left up to genetics or gender. If he struggles to keep up, it doesn't mean he is inherently clueless; it just means he needs to be explicitly taught the skills that might not come naturally to him yet. Knowing that boys often have this energy and pattern of behavior, here is how she can proactively help him bridge the gap between "being a boy" and succeeding in school: **1. Teach him the "boring" skills explicitly** She shouldn't assume he knows how to keep his desk clean, pack his backpack, or remember his homework. She can make checklists with pictures for him to follow until it becom